7.0,"cells":713,"bb":[116.9,29.5,121.9,34.8],"kind":"gain","seed":null,"slow":false},{"pid":80,"lo":-450.0,"hi":-427.0,"cells":15,"bb":[116.3,29.6,122.0,31.9],"kind":"loss","seed":null,"slow":false},{"pid":80,"lo":-404.0,"hi":-370.5,"cells":35,"bb":[118.3,33.1,120.8,35.5],"kind":"gain","seed":null,"slow":false},{"pid":80,"lo":-404.0,"hi":-370.5,"cells":51,"bb":[117.8,33.9,120.1,34.8],"kind":"loss","seed":null,"slow":false},{"pid":81,"lo":-404.0,"hi":-394.0,"cells":9803,"bb":[72.1,36.6,93.4,46.5],"kind":"appear","seed":null,"slow":false},{"pid":82,"lo":-500.0,"hi":-490.0,"cells":55,"bb":[-97.4,16.8,-96.3,17.7],"kind":"appear","seed":null,"slow":false},{"pid":83,"lo":-404.0,"hi":-394.0,"cells":578,"bb":[111.1,35.4,117.1,40.1],"kind":"appear","seed":null,"slow":false},{"pid":84,"lo":-450.0,"hi":-427.0,"cells":61,"bb":[113.2,34.8,115.2,35.7],"kind":"gain","seed":null,"slow":false},{"pid":86,"lo":-450.0,"hi":-427.0,"cells":3,"bb":[117.5,34.9,117.7,35.1],"kind":"gain","seed":null,"slow":false},{"pid":86,"lo":-450.0,"hi":-427.0,"cells":11,"bb":[117.0,35.2,118.1,35.6],"kind":"loss","seed":null,"slow":false},{"pid":86,"lo":-404.0,"hi":-381.0,"cells":35,"bb":[117.2,34.9,118.2,35.5],"kind":"end","seed":null,"slow":false}],"pacing":"Playback slows only while a polity delivered by a route on this map gains, or first takes, at least 100 cells (0.1 degree). Measured in cells, never screen pixels, so the pace never depends on the view.","renames":[{"from":30,"to":46,"year":-480,"cells":44},{"from":56,"to":69,"year":-318,"cells":1080}],"sourceNotes":{"Southern Song":[{"from":1028,"to":1138,"short":"SOURCE LABEL DISPUTED","html":"<strong>THE SOURCE'S NAME FOR THIS GROUND IS WRONG, AND THIS MAP DRAWS IT AS THE SOURCE DOES.</strong> Cliopatria v0.2.0 ends its Northern Song in 1027 and names the same ground Southern Song from 1028. The Southern Song is conventionally dated from 1127, after the Jurchen Jin took Kaifeng; the name refers to that loss of the north. The source's own records disagree with its label: its three Southern Song shapes for 1028–1138 carry no Seshat polity ID and keep the Northern Song's extent (the 1028 shape is identical to its last Northern Song shape; the later two differ by under 2%), reaching about 39°N and containing Kaifeng, while the Seshat ID for the Southern Song (cn_southern_song_dyn) first appears on its 1139 shape, which stops south of 35°N and no longer contains Kaifeng. Nothing happened to the Song in 1028: the change of name and colour here is the source's, not history's. It is left as the source has it, because this map does not silently correct its sources."}],"Later Zhou":[{"from":-750,"to":-257,"short":"SOURCE LABEL DISPUTED","html":"<strong>THE SOURCE'S NAME FOR THIS GROUND IS WRONG, AND THIS MAP DRAWS IT AS THE SOURCE DOES.</strong> Cliopatria v0.2.0 names this ground “Later Zhou” and links it to the Later Zhou dynasty, which ruled northern China in 951–960 CE. Its ground, around Luoyang, and its dates, 750–257 BCE, are those of the Eastern Zhou royal domain — and the source's own records say so: from 450 BCE the same shapes carry the Seshat ID cn_eastern_zhou_warring_states. The label is wrong; the shapes and dates are the source's own. It is left as the source has it, because this map does not silently correct its sources."}],"Great Yuan":[{"from":-110,"to":50,"short":"SOURCE LINK DISPUTED","html":"<strong>THE SOURCE LINKS THIS GROUND TO THE WRONG POLITY, AND THIS MAP DRAWS IT AS THE SOURCE DOES.</strong> Cliopatria v0.2.0 links “Great Yuan” to the Yuan dynasty — the Mongol dynasty that ruled China from 1271 to 1368 CE. Its ground, the Ferghana valley, and its dates, from 110 BCE, are those of Dayuan, the Ferghana kingdom that Han Chinese records call 大宛, “Great Yuan”. The name is a literal translation of the right polity; the links point to the wrong one. It is left as the source has it, because this map does not silently correct its sources."}],"Macedonian Empire":[{"from":-675,"to":-327,"short":"SOURCE LABEL DISPUTED","html":"<strong>THE SOURCE CALLS THIS GROUND AN EMPIRE CENTURIES BEFORE IT WAS ONE, AND THIS MAP DRAWS IT AS THE SOURCE DOES.</strong> Cliopatria v0.2.0 names every shape of Macedon from 675 BCE “Macedonian Empire”. Its own link for them is the ancient <em>kingdom</em> of Macedonia, and its own Seshat ID for the empire (gr_macedonian_emp) first appears on its shape for 326 BCE. Macedon was a kingdom for more than three centuries before Alexander's conquests, begun in 334 BCE, made it an empire. It is left as the source has it, because this map does not silently correct its sources."}],"Athenian Coalition":[{"from":-323,"to":-257,"short":"SOURCE LINK DISPUTED","html":"<strong>THE SOURCE'S LINK FOR THIS COALITION DOES NOT MATCH ITS DATES, AND THIS MAP DRAWS IT AS THE SOURCE DOES.</strong> Cliopatria v0.2.0 links “Athenian Coalition” to the Peloponnesian War (431–404 BCE), which ended eighty years before this coalition's first dated shape. Its two shapes are dated 323–319 and 264–257 BCE, which fall around the Lamian War (323–322) and the Chremonidean War (267–261) — both Athenian-led coalitions against Macedon. The source does not say which it means. It is left as the source has it, because this map does not silently correct its sources."}]},"routeSeeds":[{"polity":"Norman Italy","year":1034,"route":"r135","lat":40.97,"lon":14.21,"place":"Aversa","arrival":1030.0},{"polity":"Duchy of Normandy","year":1066,"route":"r136","lat":50.82,"lon":0.33,"place":"Pevensey","arrival":1066.7397260273972},{"polity":"Norman England","year":1072,"route":"r136","lat":50.82,"lon":0.33,"place":"Pevensey","arrival":1066.7397260273972}],"territoryCells":{},"method":"Input: the shipped act files (data/acts: Cliopatria v0.2.0, simplified 0.25 deg, 2 dp) for this act and the one\nbefore it (for predecessor shapes), and the source geojson only for MemberOf (the composite a polity belongs to).\nOutput: data/borders/act_<from>_<to>.json (header) and .bin (grid and timed changes).\n\nGRID. 0.1-degree cells over the act's extent. A cell's state is the SET of real (leaf) polities drawing it at that\nmoment. Composite \"(...)\" records are not drawn (C8); they name the occupier in the tooltip.\n\nTIME. A Cliopatria record is a dated shape, valid from its first year to the end of its last. A spread is the change\nfrom one record of a polity to its next. It starts at the new record's first year and is complete by the middle of\nthat record's span, so the map never shows ground before the source dates it, and every dated shape is reached\ninside its own window.\n - GAINED cells arrive in order of distance measured through the new shape (8-neighbour shortest path; ground outside\n   the shape costs 4x, so colour flows along coasts rather than jumping water), from the previous shape, from a\n   delivering route's landing point, or, for a first appearance with neither, from the point farthest inside the\n   shape. The front is eased: slow first cell, accelerates, settles, arrives.\n - LOST cells recede in the same window, farthest from the remaining shape first, unless another polity's spread\n   over that ground has begun by then, in which case they go exactly when that front arrives (taken, not emptied).\n - A polity that ends recedes after its last year, over half its last span, farthest from its core first.\n - Where the source draws two polities on one cell at one time, the cell holds both and is drawn striped.","attribution":{"source":"Cliopatria v0.2.0 (Seshat Global History Databank)","release":"GitHub tag v0.2.0 — https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria/releases/tag/v0.2.0","release_archive_sha256":"04e9f0d8ecb1d18a0cbb7d37a0b963a2c0ca4ebc2935708d27cc5d5131e4d5b8","internal_label_note":"The payload self-identifies as \"cliopatria_polities_only_v012\"; cite the release tag, not that label.","licence":"CC BY 4.0 — https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","citation":"Bennett, J.S., Mutch, E., Tollefson, A. et al. (2025). Cliopatria — A geospatial database of world-wide political entities from 3400BCE to 2024CE. Scientific Data 12, 247. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-025-04516-9","changes_made":"Changes were made. Features filtered to those overlapping this act window; polygons simplified with Douglas-Peucker at 0.25 degrees (topology preserved), invalid geometries repaired, and coordinates snapped to a 0.01 degree grid (2 decimal places); properties reduced to Name (n), FromYear (f), ToYear (t) and Wikidata Q-ID (w)."},"changes_made_here":"Changes were made: this act's polygons (simplified at 0.25 degrees when the acts were cut) were rasterised to 0.1-degree cells; composite records are not drawn; the growth and retreat between two dated shapes is computed by this map (method above) and is not in the source.","bin_bytes":99839,"bin_sha256":"e8804186b5ad8c6b50cc68f7bb49e3695bc21b88c71e9241bb527f5bef861b4d","stats":{"max_owners_on_one_cell":5,"cells_clipped_to_three_owners":1570,"taken_cells":122356,"groups":3581,"change_runs":27484,"base_runs":3119,"notes":{"present_before_window":49,"renames":2}},"pairSince":[[1,7,-609.375],[2,3,-743.5],[2,50,-743.3125],[2,51,-742.9375],[2,83,-745.25],[2,91,-745.1875],[3,50,-740.6875],[3,66,-742.0],[3,85,-741.75],[6,7,-519.625],[6,26,-546.5],[15,24,-500],[21,22,-594.625],[26,40,-670.375],[26,55,-550.0],[28,34,-500],[34,35,-500],[38,48,-687.5],[45,51,-746.25],[45,71,-516.0],[47,51,-742.875],[47,59,-746.125],[50,66,-743.5],[50,84,-745.0625],[50,85,-743.25],[50,95,-744.4375],[51,59,-745.5625],[51,63,-744.125],[51,66,-500],[51,83,-744.375],[59,66,-746.4375],[66,71,-740.875],[66,79,-746.375],[66,84,-744.375],[71,72,-744.0625],[72,83,-743.0625],[72,91,-742.375],[79,84,-740.0],[83,91,-744.25],[84,85,-744.0]]}